Description:
  Adds OAuth 2.1 sign-in for MCP clients (the MCP Inspector, Claude, ...) on
  top of Clave's email-code login. Needs maquina:clave; run maquina:mcp
  afterwards for the /mcp server the tokens unlock.

  Clients identify themselves with a Client ID Metadata Document: the
  client_id is an https URL, fetched by a guarded plain-Ruby fetcher that
  refuses private and loopback addresses, literal IPs, redirects, large or
  slow answers. There is no dynamic client registration and no client
  secret; PKCE S256 is required. Installs OauthClient, OauthAuthorizationCode,
  AccessToken (digests only; refresh tokens rotate and are bound to their
  client) and ConnectedApplication; the RFC 8414 and RFC 9728 discovery
  documents; GET/POST /oauth/authorize (the consent screen), POST
  /oauth/token and POST /oauth/revoke; a Connected apps page to revoke a
  client; app/models/mcp.rb with the Mcp.enabled_for?(user) gate the app
  owns; EN/ES locales, fixtures, a test helper and tests. With
  maquina:rack_attack present, /mcp and /oauth/token leave the per-IP budget
  and token requests are throttled per client. Anything it cannot place
  safely is listed after the install instead of being forced.

Options:
  --prefix PATH    Where Connected apps lives (default /settings, so
                   /settings/connected_apps)
  --flag NAME      Gate MCP behind a feature flag read by Mcp.enabled_for?
                   (credentials features.NAME, then ENV[NAME upcased])
  --skip-views     Endpoints only; bring your own consent and Connected apps views
  --quiet          No post-install message

Examples:
  rails g maquina:oauth
  rails g maquina:oauth --prefix /account
  rails g maquina:oauth --flag beta_mcp
